on this sunny day in tunis thousands  came out to protest against their  president  they were responding to a call for  action from a group known as citizens  against the coup  they believe president kai saeed broke  the law by suspending parliament and  sacking the prime minister in july and  they want him to step down  the future in tunisia is really scary to  me  for now we don't know what are we  gonna have as an institution we don't  have a parliament  we don't have  democracy in our country i am really  scared  protesters used a familiar chant the  people want the removal of the president  they also urge security forces to remain  neutral in this constitutional crisis  many support anahda the largest block in  the suspended parliament  situation is very critical and it is  time for mr kaiser to start listening to  the people and sitting on the table and  getting a  dialogue with those who are living with  him in this country mr kaisaid is there  because he is the president of tunisian  republic so he is president of all  tunisians not only of those who are  there to support him president kai syed  is now ruling by decree after assuming  nearly all executive power he's pledged  to fight corruption and revive a long  stagnant economy he's defended his move  saying it was to save the country but  his critics call it a coup  and while saeed broke ground by  appointing the first female prime  minister he's yet to provide a road map  out of this crisis and she's yet to name  a government  a week ago it was the president's  supporters who were on this very street  endorsing the policies and power grab so  many are against  thousands rallied in tunis and other  cities according to interior ministry  figures yet saeed was filmed telling his  prime minister designate that 1.8  million had turned out nationwide to  back him  the discrepancy is perhaps a reflection  of how divided tunisia has become  andrew chappelle al jazeera
 

